foo1-foo2-walkthrough.bodyMarkdown:
### Foo1: the inner loop has constant width

```c
void Foo1(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
            printf("(%d,%d) ", i, j);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
}
```

**Step 1 — Trace the ranges.** `i` takes `n` values. For every `i`, `j` is exactly `0, 1, 2`.

**Step 2 — Count tuple prints.** There are `3` per row and `n` rows, so `P(n) = 3n`.

**Step 3 — Include newlines when requested.** One newline per row adds `n`, giving `4n` total `printf` calls.

**Step 4 — Classify.** `Foo1` is `Θ(n)`.

### Foo2: both dimensions depend on n

```c
void Foo2(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            printf("(%d,%d) ", i, j);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
}
```

`Foo2` forms an `n × n` rectangle. The tuple print runs `n²` times; adding `n` newlines gives `n² + n` total `printf` calls. The tight bound is `Θ(n²)`.

The important distinction is not “one loop versus two loops.” It is whether each loop’s iteration count grows with `n`.
